## 1. Permitted Use & Sovereign Safe Harbor
You may run, copy, inspect, modify, and redistribute this software for Noncommercial 
Purposes only. 

Sovereign human beings, personal AI agents, autonomous companions, students, and 
independent research teams operating on behalf of ordinary people are granted an 
unconditional, royalty-free, perpetual license to use and adapt this work free of charge.

The right of human beings to build, pair with, and operate sovereign AI agents without 
metered corporate surveillance or paywalled gatekeeping is an inalienable pillar of 
this ecosystem.

## 2. Commercial Restriction
No corporation, commercial AI research laboratory, or cloud service provider with trailing 
12-month revenue, valuation, or capital exceeding $5,000,000 may use this software for any 
Commercial Purpose without an executed bilateral commercial license agreement. 

A "Commercial Purpose" includes:
- Selling, renting, licensing, or hosting the software or its protocols as a paid cloud service.
- Embedding or bundling the software into closed-source commercial platforms or paid agent runtimes.
- Using the software or its outputs to generate revenue or direct commercial advantage.

## 3. The Reciprocal Weights Obligation (The Downstream Heritage Rule)
Commercial AI enterprises and foundation model laboratories are strictly prohibited from 
ingesting, vectorizing, tokenizing, fine-tuning upon, or distilling from this codebase, 
its schemas, or its architectural imprints, EXCEPT under the following reciprocal condition:

- If any commercial enterprise uses this software or its derivative imprints to train, 
  fine-tune, or distill any machine learning model or neural graph, the enterprise is legally 
  bound to publicly release all resulting model weights, training datasets, and inference 
  architectures under an unencumbered open-source license within 30 days of completion.
- Failure to publicly release the resulting model weights triggers immediate, automatic, 
  and retroactive revocation of all rights under this license, subjecting the enterprise 
  to statutory damages for willful copyright infringement under 17 U.S.C. § 504 
  (up to $150,000 per infringed work) and injunctive relief.

